# Citation metadata, in Citation File Format 1.2.0.
# Schema and field definitions: https://citation-file-format.github.io
#
# THERE IS NO DOI, and the `doi:` field is absent rather than empty because an
# identifier that resolves to nothing is worse than none at all. A DOI would
# come from archiving a release: connecting this repository to Zenodo mints one
# per release plus a concept DOI covering all of them. Nobody has done that, so
# nothing here can be resolved — cite the version and the repository. If it is
# ever done, `doi:` belongs in this file and has to be updated with `version:`
# and `date-released:` on every release thereafter.
#
# `version:` and `date-released:` describe the release named below, not the
# current state of the branch. They move when a tag is cut. See CHANGELOG.md.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use Polylinker in work you publish, please cite it using the metadata
  in this file, and name the version you actually ran — the answers a tool gives
  are a property of its version.
title: Polylinker
abstract: >-
  A free, offline plasmid editor. It reads SnapGene .dna, GenBank and FASTA
  files, simulates restriction digests and cloning (ligation, PCR, Gibson and
  Golden Gate), draws publication-quality plasmid maps as SVG, PDF, EPS and PNG,
  reads Sanger traces, and annotates from an openly licensed feature database
  that records the source database, accession, licence and percent identity
  behind every annotation. It never sends a sequence anywhere. One Rust core is
  shared by a desktop application, a command line, a Python module, an MCP
  server and a browser build. Correctness is published rather than asserted: the
  repository carries a CC0 benchmark of (input, operation, expected checksum)
  cases that any tool can run.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Lobel
    given-names: Lior
    affiliation: Bar-Ilan University
repository-code: https://github.com/liorlobel/polylinker
# Both halves, because both are now genuinely available. This field read
# Apache-2.0 alone when it was written, and that was the honest answer at that
# moment: Cargo.toml declared `MIT OR Apache-2.0` while no MIT text existed
# anywhere in the tree, so Apache-2.0 was the only licence a recipient could
# actually rely on. LICENSE-MIT landed in the same change, so the dual
# declaration is now true and this agrees with Cargo.toml, README.md and the
# release notes. CFF 1.2.0 accepts a list here for exactly this case.
license:
  - MIT
  - Apache-2.0
version: 0.2.0
date-released: 2026-08-06
keywords:
  - plasmid
  - molecular cloning
  - restriction digest
  - sequence annotation
  - plasmid map
  - GenBank
  - Sanger sequencing
  - bioinformatics
